.\" novi is by Dave W Plummer, written for 2.11BSD on a split-I/D PDP-11:
.\"     https://x.com/davepl1968/status/2080867382105710812
.\" This page is new: novi is not a v7 command, so there was none to correct.
.\" See README.md beside this file for what the port to this machine changed.
.\" It names Cyrillic letters, so read it with `nroff -Kutf8 -man novi.1'.
.TH NOVI 1
.SH NAME
novi \- full-screen text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B novi
[
file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Novi
edits
.I file
on the screen: the text is displayed, the cursor is moved over it with the arrow keys, and
what is typed appears where the cursor is.  If
.I file
does not exist it is created when the buffer is written; with no argument the buffer starts
empty and
.B ^O
asks for a name.
.PP
The commands are control characters, and the two lines at the foot of the screen list the
common ones.  The line above them shows messages and asks questions.  The line at the top
shows the file name, whether the buffer has been changed, and whether typing inserts
.RB ( [INS] )
or replaces
.RB ( [OVR] ).
.PP
Cursor motion:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 16
arrow keys
one character or one line
.TP
.B ^B \ ^F
left, right
.TP
.B ^P \ ^N
up, down
.TP
.B ^A \ ^E
start, end of line
.TP
Home, End
the same
.TP
.B ^Y \ ^V
one screenful up, down
.TP
PgUp, PgDn
the same
.PD
.PP
Changing the text:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 16
.B ^D
delete the character under the cursor; Del does the same
.TP
.B ^H
delete the character before it; the erase key does the same
.TP
Ins
switch between inserting and replacing
.TP
.B ^K
cut from the cursor to the end of the line.  Struck again with nothing in between, it adds
the next line to what was already cut instead of replacing it
.TP
.B ^U
insert everything
.B ^K
has cut
.TP
.B ^R
insert a file at the cursor
.PD
.PP
Everything else:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 16
.B ^G
a page describing these keys
.TP
.B ^O
write the buffer out.  The current name is offered and may be edited
.TP
.B ^W
search forward for a string, wrapping round the end of the buffer
.TP
.B ^C
report the line, the column and the character position
.TP
.B ^X
leave.  A changed buffer is asked about first
.PD
.PP
When
.I novi
asks a question, printable characters answer it, the erase key rubs one out, a newline
accepts and
.B ^C
or ESC cancels.
.PP
Tabs are shown as spaces to the next eighth column.  Other control characters are shown as
.BR ? .
Text is treated as UTF\-8 throughout: a Cyrillic letter is two bytes and one column, and
may be typed into the buffer, into a file name and into a search string alike.
.SH "THE TERMINAL"
.I Novi
writes ANSI/VT100 escape sequences and reads them back from the arrow and function keys.
It consults no terminal database:
.IR termcap (5)
is not read and
.B $TERM
is not looked at.  The console this system boots on is a Consul\-254 typewriter, which is a
suitable terminal only because the simulator's line is
.I raw8
and passes the escapes through to whatever is at the far end.
.PP
There is no window-size call in this kernel, so the screen is taken to be 24 lines of 80
columns unless
.B $LINES
and
.B $COLUMNS
say otherwise.
.PP
While
.I novi
runs, the terminal is in RAW mode with echo off.
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP 20
/tmp/noviL*
the text before the cursor
.TP
/tmp/noviR*
the text after it, stored in reverse
.TP
/tmp/noviC*
what
.B ^K
has cut
.PD
.PP
All three are unlinked as soon as they are opened, so they disappear when
.I novi
does, however it goes.  The document is never held in memory; the two halves together take
at most twice the size of the file being edited.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Novi
refuses a file name longer than 255 characters, and says so if the terminal or the scratch
files cannot be set up.  Everything else is reported on the message line and the editor
carries on.
.SH AUTHOR
Dave W Plummer.
This page and the port to this machine are not his.
.SH BUGS
.B ^O
writes the file in place with
.IR creat (2),
so a write that fails part of the way through leaves the file truncated.  There is no
.IR rename (2)
on this system to make the replacement atomic;
.IR ed (1)'s
.B w
has the same exposure.
.PP
A
.I novi
killed by a signal it cannot catch leaves the terminal in RAW mode with echo off.
.IR Stty (1)
is the way back.
.PP
A lone ESC is not acted on until another key is struck, there being no way to time out a
read.
.PP
The interactive part of
.I novi
has no automated test.  See README.md beside the source.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ed(1), stty(1)
